From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Cook <jacobmcook1984@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mmc: sd: retry a failed resume after a power cycle

_mmc_sd_resume() re-initialises the card and hands the result back to
mmc_sd_runtime_resume(), which logs it and returns 0. The PM core is
therefore told the resume succeeded even when the card was never
brought back, and every request already queued against it waits
forever.

That is not theoretical. Cycling suspend/resume quickly on a GKD
Pixel2 (PX30S), the boot card is still busy when the host re-init
starts:

  dwmmc_rockchip ff370000.mmc: Busy; trying anyway
  mmc_host mmc0: Timeout sending command (cmd 0x202000 arg 0x0 status 0x80202000)
  mmc_host mmc0: Timeout sending command (cmd 0x202000 arg 0x0 status 0x80202000)
  mmc0: error -95 doing runtime resume

dw_mmc waits up to 500 ms for the card to release DAT0 and then issues
the command regardless, so CMD0 times out and initialisation fails. A
card is entitled to be busy for longer than that, so this can happen
with any card - it just needs to be finishing internal work when the
host comes back.

Afterwards jbd2, the writeback worker and the block queue sit in
uninterruptible sleep forever, and every process that touches the
filesystem joins them. On a boot medium that takes the whole machine
down: userspace stops responding while the kernel carries on happily
answering pings, and even sshd cannot get far enough to print a
banner.

Give the card a second chance from a clean slate. The failing attempt
has already spent several seconds in command timeouts, so by the time
the retry runs the card has had ample time to become ready, and the
power cycle resets the host controller state machine as well.

Raising dw_mmc's busy timeout was considered instead and rejected: that
poll is readl_poll_timeout_atomic() and dw_mci_start_command() runs
under host->lock, so a longer wait means spinning longer in atomic
context.

--- a/drivers/mmc/core/sd.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/sd.c
@@ -1779,6 +1779,28 @@
 
 	mmc_power_up(host, host->card->ocr);
 	err = mmc_sd_init_card(host, host->card->ocr, host->card);
+	if (err) {
+		/*
+		 * The card may still be busy from before the suspend: a card
+		 * finishing internal housekeeping can hold DAT0 low for longer
+		 * than the host controller is prepared to wait, and the host
+		 * may then issue the initialisation command anyway and time
+		 * out. Give it one more attempt from a clean slate.
+		 *
+		 * Without this the card is left uninitialised while the PM
+		 * core is told the resume succeeded, so every request queued
+		 * against it hangs forever and takes down every task that
+		 * touches the filesystem - on a boot medium that wedges the
+		 * whole machine.
+		 */
+		pr_warn("%s: resume failed (%d), retrying after a power cycle\n",
+			mmc_hostname(host), err);
+		mmc_power_cycle(host, host->card->ocr);
+		err = mmc_sd_init_card(host, host->card->ocr, host->card);
+		if (err)
+			pr_err("%s: card could not be re-initialised on resume (%d)\n",
+			       mmc_hostname(host), err);
+	}
 	mmc_card_clr_suspended(host->card);
 
 out:
